You can now read agent conversations alongside their[traces](https://mastra.ai/docs/studio/observability#traces) in Mastra Studio. Open an agent trace to see its messages next to the steps that produced them. Then open the full thread to see the whole conversation in order.


When you debug an agent's behavior, you want to follow the story of what the user asked, what the agent decided, and which tools it called. The longer the agent runs, the harder it gets to find the right step. A single session can span dozens of messages. Thread view lets you inspect the trace in the context of the whole conversation.


Your browser does not support the video tag.


Agent activity is usually traced as a tree of nested steps, model calls, and timestamps. Even though a tree view is precise, it's difficult to interpret. You have to reconstruct the conversation to understand what happened. With the thread view, it's the other way around.


You start from the messages and when something looks wrong, you dig into the steps behind it. It's easier to read, follow and navigate. Thread view also helps subject-matter experts who need to review an agent's behavior but didn't build the agent.


## Get started


If your agent already uses memory and persists traces with` MastraStorageExporter` , no additional setup is required. Thread view works with its existing stored traces.


If you start from scratch, install Mastra observability, memory, and storage:


Terminal


```text
npm   install   mastra@latest   @mastra/core   @mastra/observability   @mastra/memory   @mastra/libsql
```


note


Requires` mastra@1.33.0` or later, added in[PR #25812](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/25812) .


Thread view appears for agent traces that belong to a thread. Give your agent[memory](https://mastra.ai/docs/memory/overview) and persist traces with the` MastraStorageExporter` :


src/mastra/index.ts


```text
import   {   Mastra   }   from   "  @mastra/core/mastra  "  ;
import   {   Agent   }   from   "  @mastra/core/agent  "  ;
import   {   Memory   }   from   "  @mastra/memory  "  ;
import   {   LibSQLStore   }   from   "  @mastra/libsql  "  ;
import   {   Observability,   MastraStorageExporter   }   from   "  @mastra/observability  "  ;


const   supportAgent   =   new   Agent  ({
id:   "  support-agent  "  ,
name:   "  Support Agent  "  ,
instructions:   /* ... */  ,
model:   "  openai/gpt-5.6-astra  "  ,
tools: {   /* ... */   },
memory:   new   Memory  ()
});


export   const   mastra   =   new   Mastra  ({
agents: { supportAgent },
storage:   new   LibSQLStore  ({ id:   "  storage  "  , url:   "  file:./mastra.db  "   }),
observability:   new   Observability  ({
configs: {
default: {
serviceName:   "  mastra  "  ,
exporters: [  new   MastraStorageExporter  ()]
}
}
})
});
```


Call the agent with a` thread` and` resource` . Each call becomes one turn in the thread, and one trace in Studio:


```text
const   agent   =   mastra.  getAgent  (  "  supportAgent  "  );


await   agent.  generate  (  "  What's the status of my order?  "  ,   {
memory: { thread:   "  thread-123  "  , resource:   "  user-456  "   }
});
```


Run` mastra dev` , open **Traces** in Studio, and select an agent run. The conversation for that turn appears beside the span tree.


Hover over any message and select **Highlight spans** to see the steps behind it with the rest of the tree faded.


Select **Open full thread** to see the whole conversation.


For more information and full configuration options, see:


- [Studio observability](https://mastra.ai/docs/studio/observability)
- [Tracing overview](https://mastra.ai/docs/observability/tracing/overview)
- [Memory overview](https://mastra.ai/docs/memory/overview)
